# Custom annotations: annotation_custom

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`annotation_custom()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/annotation_custom.html), in the
order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example
Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is
also a list of what remains.

`annotation_custom()` draws a `grid` grob, one of the graphical objects R's grid graphics are
made of, such as a table or an inset plot, in a rectangle given in data coordinates, the same in
every panel. It does not change the scales, so the axes do not grow to fit it, and no setting or
mapping of the plot changes it; `-Inf` and `Inf` stretch it to the panel's edges. Giggly has none
of it yet.

## A grob over the whole panel

```r
# Dummy plot
df <- data.frame(x = 1:10, y = 1:10)
base <- ggplot(df, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_blank() +
  theme_bw()

# Full panel annotation
base + annotation_custom(
  grob = grid::roundrectGrob(),
  xmin = -Inf, xmax = Inf, ymin = -Inf, ymax = Inf
)
```

`geom_blank()` draws nothing, but its data still sets the scales.

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_blank()`, `theme_bw()`, `annotation_custom()`, and `grid` grobs.

## An inset plot

```r
# Inset plot
df2 <- data.frame(x = 1 , y = 1)
g <- ggplotGrob(ggplot(df2, aes(x, y)) +
  geom_point() +
  theme(plot.background = element_rect(colour = "black")))
base +
  annotation_custom(grob = g, xmin = 1, xmax = 10, ymin = 8, ymax = 10)
```

`ggplotGrob()` turns a whole plot into a grob, to be drawn inside another.

Not in Giggly yet: `ggplotGrob()` and `grid` grobs, changing the theme, here to border the inset,
`annotation_custom()`, and the `geom_blank()` and `theme_bw()` of `base`.
